within just two years of launching this  platform became the world's largest  social media network eventually  surpassing over 100 million users and  within three years this website became  the most visited website in the us  surpassing even google and yahoo given  this explosive growth i don't think  you'd be surprised to hear that this  platform was acquired for 580 million  dollars just two years after being  founded hearing this you might be  thinking that i'm describing the roaring  success of instagram  [Music]  but that was actually the story of  myspace and the part that we've yet to  discuss is its equally explosive  downfall in mid 2008 facebook would  surpass myspace in terms of user account  and within the next five years myspace's  user count would crumble from 115  million users to 36 million and the  worst part was that myspace would be  resold to another buyer for just 35  million dollars which is only six  percent of their initial 580 million  dollar buyout but that's not even  surprising given that virtually no one  uses myspace today  on the other hand instagram never became  the world's largest social media  platform  however its growth was just as explosive  as myspace within three years of  launching it instagram boasted an  astonishing of 100 million users and it  didn't take long for facebook to buy out  instagram for a whopping of 1 billion  dollars but as we saw with myspace rapid  adoption and doesn't necessarily  indicate long-term success and users can  dissipate faster than they came on this  isn't to say that instagram is going to  explode in spectacular fashion within  the next couple of years but at the same  time we're starting to see red flags pop  up that indicate that instagram may  share its final destination with myspace  so here's the top reasons that instagram  is doomed to become the next myspace  starting with the most concrete evidence  we have instagram's user base  while their overall user count is yet to  show any signs of slowing down something  that's worrisome is the demographics of  this user base and especially their age  if we take a look at instagram's age  demographics we'll see that only eight  percent of their user base is 13 to 17  year olds this means that the number of  high schoolers that are using instagram  is equivalent to the number of 45 to 54  year olds that are using instagram  this may not seem like a problem at  first glance but when we take a look at  their competitors demographics we'll see  just how much instagram is lacking take  snapchat for example  13 to 17 year olds make up just over 20  of their user base while 18 to 24 year  olds make up nearly 40  meanwhile 50 plus year olds make up just  3.7 percent which is 8 times less than  the 13 to 17 year old user base if we  take a look at tick tock we'll see a  demographic that's even more skewed  towards younger people tick tock's  largest user base is 10 to 19 year olds  who make up a quarter of their user base  something else to note is that even  though 10 to 19 year olds are the  largest demographic tech talk is quite  popular amongst other age groups as well  the reason this is a problem for  instagram is that it's concrete evidence  that they're not appealing to newer  generations like they used to this isn't  to say that teenagers aren't using  social media social media use amongst  teenagers is higher than ever before  in fact 97 percent of 13 to 17 year olds  use social media instagram is simply not  their top choice based on the  demographic numbers they far prefer  snapchat and tick tock meanwhile  instagram's demographic numbers are  starting to look more and more like  facebooks facebook is extremely  unpopular amongst 13 to 17 year olds in  fact there's more 55 to 64 year olds  that use facebook than 13 to 17 year  olds even the 65 plus demographic is  comparable to the 13 to 17 year olds and  facebook now there are some benefits  that come with an aging demographic  first of all your user base actually has  money to spend meaning that you can  charge advertisers a much higher amount  and secondly you probably won't have  stupid trends go viral on your platform  like dbs licks but that's pretty much  where the benefits end as people get  older they tend to spend less time on  social media not only because they have  more responsibilities but also because  they no longer feel the need to live for  the gram they tend to prioritize family  kids and close friends over keeping up  some sort of online persona while this  is probably good for them this is  terrible for social media platforms your  user base slowly evolves from teenagers  and young adults who spend three to four  hours per day on your platform to middle  age people who check in once every few  days or weeks i don't think i need to  explain why this is terrible for  business and unless instagram starts to  get newer generations on board this is  where they're eventually headed  aside from lackluster performance  amongst gen z instagram has devolved  into becoming copycats at first  instagram's parent company meta tried to  acquire their way to the top they spend  ludicrous amounts on acquisitions like  instagram and whatsapp but not every  business owner can be persuaded to sell  by just money for example facebook  offered 3 billion in cash to take over  snapchat way back in 2013 but snapchat's  ceo evan spiegel refused and when meta  realized that they couldn't acquire  every company they liked they just  started ripping off every company they  liked we saw instagram introduced  stories which were awfully similar to  snapchat stories we also saw instagram  launch wheels which were basically a  rip-off of tick tock now we can't give  instagram too much heat for this because  basically every social media platform  has resorted to doing this i mean just  take a look at youtube stories and  shorts  the problem with this however is that  such shameless copying leaves these  platforms with no originality as they  all basically become the same thing not  to mention it none of these copied  features are all that great at  attracting new users they may attract  new creators who are looking to leverage  the low competition but it rarely  attracts new users i mean if you don't  have tik-tok or instagram and you want  to watch short form videos why would you  download instagram over tik tok  especially when they're both free it  doesn't make any sense instagram's  demographic numbers prove this without a  shadow of doubt meta and youtube are  well aware of this as well and they're  not trying to attract new users with  such features per se  rather they're trying to preserve their  existing base and minimize losses in  watch time but as they spend so much  time on copying features and preserving  watch time they often forget about what  appealed to users in the first place  this is especially a problem with  instagram because the user base has  already started to turn on them and i'm  not talking about meta's privacy  concerns or political involvement i'm  talking about people who are turning on  instagram because the platform is toxic  to their mental health it's no secret  that instagram is filled with  over-the-top photos instagram has  transitioned from being a place where  you share your life with your friends to  being a place where you share your  highlights with people you know to being  a place where you share doctored photos  to impress strangers instagram users are  more aware of this than ever before  which has completely warped their  perception of the platform and i think  this is what meta should have focused on  they should have spent time encouraging  originality and authenticity which is  what attracted users to their platform  in the first place but instead they were  busy copying features from tiktok and  snapchat and as instagram slay morphs  into being just another social media  platform it'll just become even harder  for them to appeal to younger  generations  by this point it's no secret that one of  the main factors that's causing all  social media companies to squirm is tick  tock tick tock has taken the world by  storm and though many like to criticize  it tick tock was still the most visited  website in 2021 and given that people  only have 24 hours in a day all this  traffic has to come from somewhere and  more times than not that somewhere is  other social media platforms such as  instagram and youtube and looking  forward it doesn't seem like tick tock  dominance is going to stop anytime soon  over the years our attention spans have  only gotten smaller and smaller first  people switched from traditional media  like movies and tv shows to shorter from  online content like youtube videos and  now we're seeing them switch from  youtube videos to tick-tock shorts this  isn't to say that other forms of media  will be left behind completely but as  tick-tock continues as dominant it's  likely that other platforms will just  continue to bleed but honestly this  isn't anything new within the social  media space since the creation of social  media in the late 1990s users have  flogged from one platform to the next  first there was friendster then there  was myspace and after that we saw  facebook and twitter  at this point people switched from  computers to smartphones and we saw the  rise of instagram and whatsapp and then  we saw snapchat and now we have tick  tock  none of these platforms sit at the top  for more than a few years and they're  completely abandoned within a few  decades given that instagram was  launched in 2010 it's been nearly 12  years since the platform was launched so  it's not surprising that instagram is  approaching its peak form if it's not  already there something else to note is  that meta themselves have more or less  just accepted this fate 10 years ago  they were vigorously trying to acquire  the next big platform in order to always  stay on top but it seems like mark  zuckerberg has come to the realization  that this is simply unsustainable as a  result he's been shifting the company's  focus into vr and the metaverse he even  changed the company's name from facebook  to meta which is something you never  want to do for branding purposes  at this point mark is basically all in  on the metaverse and he's even been  telling shareholders to break for  significant losses due to the metaverse  over the next five years last year alone  meta spent ten billion dollars on the  metaverse and given mark stands i don't  think this will be changing anytime soon  so even if instagram isn't completely  consumed by new social media players it  looks like instagram will eventually be  left behind by meta themselves  in the end instagram is extremely  nostalgic to many of us because we grew  up with it we used it all throughout  middle school high school and college  and as young adults many of us still use  it on a regular basis so it might be  hard to imagine this massive platform  slowly withering away  but the thing to keep in mind is that  all popular social media platforms cross  the same stage myspace and friendster  were so nostalgic to a specific  generation as well but that didn't stop  these platforms from eventually going  down  15 years ago it probably seemed  impossible for something like myspace to  die yet here we are as instagram  continues to lose its originality and  meta shifts your focus on the new  projects it just becomes more and more  likely that newer generations abandon  instagram we're already starting to see  this amongst 13 to 17 year olds but this  will simply become even more blatant in  the next 10 to 15 years as these guys  grow up and soon enough people will be  talking about instagram like we talk  about myspace and friendster today but  that's just what i think do you think  instagram will break the trend and  survive for decades comment that down  below  also drop a like if instagram is  nostalgic for you and of 